\details{
\subsection{Model}{

Events occur in time as a homogeneous Poisson point process with rate \code{rate}. An age-depth model transforms them into an inhomogeneous Poisson point process in the stratigraphic domain, whose intensity is \eqn{\lambda(h) = \mathrm{rate} \cdot c(h)}, where \eqn{c(h)} is the condensation (time per stratigraphic increment, see \code{\link[=condensation]{condensation()}}). Hiatuses are treated as destructive: Events that occur during a hiatus are not preserved, so the intensity has no atoms.
}

\subsection{Decomposition}{

The KL divergence of the point process of \code{x} from the point process of \code{y} is
\deqn{\mathrm{KL} = \int \lambda_x \log(\lambda_x / \lambda_y) - \lambda_x + \lambda_y \, dh = \mathrm{volume} + \mathrm{position}.}
With \eqn{\Lambda = \int \lambda \, dh}, the expected number of preserved events (rate times the preserved time), and the normalized densities \eqn{p = \lambda / \Lambda},
\deqn{\mathrm{volume} = \Lambda_x \log(\Lambda_x / \Lambda_y) - \Lambda_x + \Lambda_y}
is the KL divergence between the Poisson distributed numbers of events, and depends only on how much time is preserved. The component
\deqn{\mathrm{position} = \Lambda_x \, \mathrm{shape}, \quad \mathrm{shape} = \mathrm{KL}(p_x \| p_y)}
quantifies differences in where the events are placed in the section. The shape term is the position divergence per preserved event. It is independent of \code{rate} and of the time unit, whereas \code{kl}, \code{volume}, \code{position}, and \eqn{\Lambda} scale linearly with \code{rate}.
}

\subsection{Symmetrized divergence}{

The KL divergence is not symmetric. \code{kl_reverse} is the KL divergence of the point process of \code{y} from the point process of \code{x}, and \code{kl_sym} is the symmetrized (Jeffreys) divergence
\deqn{\mathrm{kl\_sym} = \mathrm{KL}(x \| y) + \mathrm{KL}(y \| x) = \int (\lambda_x - \lambda_y) \log(\lambda_x / \lambda_y) \, dh,}
which is symmetric in \code{x} and \code{y}. The components of the reverse direction are stored in the element \code{reverse}.

All calculations are exact: Both intensities are constant between consecutive tie points of the two age-depth models, so all integrals reduce to finite sums.
}

\subsection{Zero intensities and the convention 0 log 0 = 0}{

Age-depth models have strictly increasing time tie points (see \code{\link[=is_adm]{is_adm()}}), so the condensation is strictly positive everywhere within the stratigraphic range of an age-depth model. Zero intensities therefore only occur if \code{h_range} extends beyond the range of \code{x} or \code{y}. Outside of its range, an age-depth model has no sediment and the intensity is zero. The following cases can occur:
\itemize{
\item Within the range of both models (in particular for the default \code{h_range}), all intensities are positive and all divergences are finite.
\item Where \eqn{\lambda_x = 0 < \lambda_y}, the term \eqn{\lambda_x \log(\lambda_x / \lambda_y)} is 0 (convention \eqn{0 \log 0 = 0}), and the interval contributes \eqn{\int \lambda_y \, dh} to the KL divergence: \code{y} predicts events where \code{x} predicts none, which is penalized only by their expected number.
